Z||000006||Coconut||12-14-2000||01:03 AM||||FieroLisa,<BR>How about getting some type of colored cellophane to mask over that orange plate,sorta like something you might be able to get at a Hallmark store,you know like the kinda stuff you get for gift wrapping? Either there or maybe at an arts and crafts or hobby store. I'm not sure how much heat those bulbs generate or if the plastic would withstand the heat but now that you've mentioned it, I may try to do something along the same lines. GREAT IDEA Lisa! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Cruisin' On, <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000007||2xsess||12-14-2000||07:08 AM||||When I did my white face gauges it required peeling off the old gauge face. It is the face that is colored on the backlit gauges. The orange plate makes your odometer light up orange, I had to paint it white to get mine(odometer) to light up white so I'm not so sure that it will make the gauges light up a different color. <BR>Good Luck with your experiments though.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-14-2000||07:22 AM||||Hi Lisa<P>btw didnt have any takers on your car, Id take it myself if it was a V6 auto in a min.Its been a while since I was in my dash but as I remember, u can just take out about 7 screws to remove the backside of cluster, then just take out the bulbs in back by turning them CC a 1/4 turn. Pep boys has bulbs in red, green, blue, orange I think.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||12-14-2000||10:17 AM||||Hi Lisa,<P>I can make gauge faces that will light up purple. You really need to replace the faces and get rid of the orange tint to make it work though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroLisa||12-14-2000||11:57 AM||||what should I be replacing the faces with? And how do you mean get rid of the orange tint- are you talking about the bright orange plate in the back?<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric||12-14-2000||02:03 PM||||The dash can be a real pain to take out.  I know that the guage cluster looks like it's right there, but I'm afraid you'll have to unbolt the whole interior to get at em.  I unbolt my interior about once a month, so it can't be that hard.  It took me about four hours the first time, but anymore I can't do it in about 45 minutes.<P>Autometer guages have colored caps for their gauges.  I'm using three of them in my aux. guage pod.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-14-2000||03:07 PM||||It was either posted here on the forum many moons ago, or I read it elsewhere, but some guy used an extra instrument cluster and painted the bright orange area various colors and tried using the tinted bulbs. I don't recall the exact results, but I do remember he was fairly disappointed for the most part. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||12-14-2000||03:12 PM||||You relace the faces with new faces. There is no way to remove the tint from the factory faces. If you try to cover the back with another color, it will be to dark.||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroLisa||12-14-2000||03:24 PM||||Taking the gauge cluster out really is not a big deal for me. I replaced the entire interior in my coupe, and have messed with it with stereo setup as well.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||kslish||12-14-2000||03:33 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2xsess:<BR><B>When I did my white face gauges it required peeling off the old gauge face. It is the face that is colored on the backlit gauges. The orange plate makes your odometer light up orange, I had to paint it white to get mine(odometer) to light up white so I'm not so sure that it will make the gauges light up a different color. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>2xsess is right.  Changing the orange plate to another color will only change the color of the items that are lit from reflected light such as the odometer (and maybe the 4 cyl non-backlit guages).  The GT's guages are backlit and the red-orange color is contained in the translucent numbers and hash marks themselves on the guage's face (hold a GT speedometer in front of a light bulb and you'll see what I mean).  <P>For further proof of this, just look at your GT's center console oil pressure and voltmeter guages.  No big orange plate there, but they light up red-orange due to the color being contained in the guage's translucent numbers and hash marks.<P>To actually change the color, you would need to replace the stock face by peeling off the old face (it is sort of like a thick sticker) and replace it with a custom made one that had purple translucent numbers and hash marks on it.  Super X Motorsports used to sell Fiero white-faced guage faces that could be custom ordered to lite up in various different colors but I'm not sure if they are still available (anyone know for sure?)<P>Your best bet is to try the blue bulb idea and see if that achieves the desired coloring, plus it will only cost the price of a few packs of blue bulbs.  That way the blue light might be changed to some shade of purple when it passes through the guage faces.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||ur2biteme||12-14-2000||11:01 PM||||allright guys, i've played with this before.  the 85 GT, the 4cyl coupes, and any v6 without the aux. pod (these clusters have the oil pressure gague in the tach) all share similar lighting.  i know the 85 GT has the non-backlit because the cluster i have in my car is out of an 85 GT and it is NOT backlit, unless it was retrofitted, or the backlit gagues were an option, which i'm not too sure either way.  <P>anyways, my point is that if it is the previously mentioned, and not backlit, it is lit up by reflection.  the entire black plate that covers the cluster is orange on the back.  i had good luck in repainting that plate white, and getting the desired color bulbs, so long as theyre bright enough.  i've seen several different types of bulbs that are the same size, but different part numbers,and the ones i've tried were of different brillance.  now if you want to change the backlit ones, i think you have to replace the faces themselves, but i'm not too sure because i've never messed with these.  <P>hope this helps<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000017||BAM-BAM||12-15-2000||12:16 AM|||| Lisa, Which car are you wanting to change? Are the gauges in the SC backlit or offset. I have a set of 84 gauges coming with the offset lighting. When they arrive I will do some testing with white flourescent paint and different colored bulbs, if they make them. BTW, they are GE194's not 164's and there are sixteen of them in a offset cluster. I'll let the Forum know the results. As for the 86-88 GT they are backlit and you know the story there. Hey SuperX, you out there? Maybe you could shed some "LIGHT" on this matter. <P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroLisa||12-15-2000||01:00 AM||||Well originally the car I was planning to do this to was the coupe. But now that it's for sale and I have the GT, that's the one I'm aiming for- and I haven't messed around with those gauges yet...About a year ago or so I talked with Super X, bought something from him, some moldings I think, I can't remember and I asked him about the purple backlight and I believe he said he wasn't sure if he could do it- I hope I'm wrong!<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||12-15-2000||07:39 PM||||I strongly recomend you do some testing before you actually do this with something permanent. Allot of colors won't give you adaquat lighting or can polute night vision. (There is a reason they are orange...)<P>The easiest way to test is use a thin coat of transparant nail polish or glass stain on the bulbs. (glass stain is available at most craft stores.) Paint that orange thing you all are talking about white or silver. This will give you a way out if a color you pick sucks. You can just put regular lights back in or try another color. ((The coat of color has to be thin or it will burn and you won't have any light.))<P>Celophane won't work. at least not for long. to much heat. plus it fades easy. If you go that rout use theater lighting gel sheet. It will hold it's color much better.||||reg||1
Z||000020||watdge||03-26-2001||08:14 PM||||I'm not exactly an experienced gauge painter or anything of that nature, but I have experimented with various forms of art work with lighting and the such.  One possibility for you all who want that crisp blue look may want to look into using phosphorous.  Yes, that stuff that glows in the dark.  I have thought about doing this sort of mod in my car.  It would mean that you would need to replace the orange film or sticker that the current gauge uses and then replace it with the properly painted color film.  A black light would be placed behind the gauges in the GT's, and I'm not exactly sure how the front lit 85 mph speedometers work.  But, I had thought about applying a blue shade to the numbers and redoing the whole dash in that fasion.  The nicest thing about the phosphorous is that it glows, it's not as if the light is trying to go through a filter to give the desired color.  And that eliminates the too dark look.  Anyway, if the light appeared to be too bright, then you could always dim the dash lights.  I haven't yet engaged in the process yet.. because I haven't gotten a fiero yet, but as soon as I do, I'll begin looking into the mod.  I'm pretty sure you could find phosphorous in many shades of colors and such at an art and crafts store or maybe autozone or something like that.  Anyway, just food for thought.  If you have an Aiwa cd player, and blue interior lights, then by golly, you've got a sweet looking ride from the inside out.  Now, another thought I have had is the needles.  How are they lit?  Is there a separate bulb that lights them from the center or is the light just reflected from the main light behind the cluster of gauges?  Either way, I would probably lightly coat that with a shade of white or something of that nature.  *semi-neon-glow-blue and glowing white needles* <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> lovely!  I can just see it now.  Oh well.  I'll chat with yall later.  <P>------------------<BR>Performance Enthusiest,<BR>Jeremy<p>[This message has been edited by watdge (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||3
